//
// `@FR-L-Narrow-Alias` — a stored WIDTH is a property of a NAMED type, so `size(n)` is
// written in a `type` alias and nowhere else.
//
// `Parser::parse_typedef` is the only reader of the modifier; inline, the parser simply never
// looked for it, so `a: integer limit(-100, 100) size(1)` on a struct field drew FOUR errors,
// none of which named `size` — `Expect token )`, `Tuple types require at least 2 elements`,
// `Expect token }` — and the reader had to work out which was theirs.  It is refused by name
// now, and the modifier CONSUMED so the declaration recovers as the plain range: one error,
// naming the modifier and its home.
//
// The rule is load-bearing beyond the message.  `data::NarrowSlot::of_slot` asserts that a
// spare-code type is never a narrow-VECTOR element, and the whole argument is that such a
// type needs an alias while a narrow vector needs none.  Until this refusal existed that was
// an accident of the parser rather than a rule (measured by loft3-ca: `formal/grammar.md` has
// no type productions at all, and `LOFT.md`'s informal grammar puts `size` in none), so an
// inline spelling that ever parsed would have walked into the assertion with nothing to warn
// it.
//
// The CONTROL is the alias spelling, which must keep working — the stdlib's own `type i32 =
// integer size(4)` is that shape, and a refusal that reached it stopped the standard library
// parsing (measured, while building this).
//
// @falsified-at: fae64225f — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/167-a-size-is-written-in-a-type-alias.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// HOLDS: leak, panic and free-refusal are clean on both trees and both backends, and BOTH
//   backends move — the refusal is a parse-time diagnostic, so unlike a runtime divergence
//   neither side is inert here.
//
// CHANNEL: the expected diagnostics, one per inline `size`.

type Ok1 = integer limit(-100, 100) size(1);   // the control: the alias spelling
type Ok2 = integer size(4);                    // the stdlib's own shape

// The annotations sit here rather than in the header: a `type` declaration does not end the
// header the way a `struct` does, so pending annotations placed above one are dropped and the
// file reports `0 expected errors` while passing.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: is a property of a NAMED type
// @EXPECT_ERROR: is a property of a NAMED type
struct Bad { a: integer limit(-100, 100) size(1) }

fn inline_local() -> integer { v: integer limit(0, 200) size(1) = 7; v }

fn main() {
  b = Bad { a: 100 };
  o: Ok1 = -100;
  p: Ok2 = 2147483647;
  println("{b.a} {o} {p} {inline_local()}");
}
